<QuestionLayout
  title="Do I need to know Rust to use Traverse?"
  description="No for the usual paths: author in plain English via traverse-capability-author, or consume via npm / CLI. Manual authoring still expects Rust."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/do-i-need-rust.html"
  category="Question"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>Short answer: <strong>no</strong> for the usual paths. You can author new capabilities in plain English, and you can consume existing ones without touching Rust. Rust only shows up if you choose the manual author path yourself.</p>

  <h2>Author in plain English — Claude skill</h2>
  <p>The official skill <code>traverse-capability-author</code> in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/claude-skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse-framework/claude-skills</a> is the LLM-assisted on-ramp. It interviews you one question at a time, checks the public (and any private) registry so you do not duplicate something that already exists, writes a JSON-Schema-backed contract, produces genuinely executable ABI-compliant WASM, and opens a human-reviewed PR. You describe the business rule in plain English; you do not have to write the Rust.</p>

  <p>Under the hood the implementation is still Rust compiled to WASM. That is what gives you determinism, sandboxing, and portability across native hosts and the browser. The skill owns that step so you do not have to.</p>

  <h2>Consume — no Rust required</h2>
  <p>If someone else already published the capability, you call it without writing Rust. JavaScript and TypeScript use the published npm package <code>traverse-embedder-web</code>. Python today shells out to <code>traverse-cli</code> with the real verb <code>capability-package execute</code> (stdlib <code>subprocess</code>) — there is no shipped Python SDK. See <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1362" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse#1362</a> for the example ticket.</p>

  <h2>Manual author path — Rust expected</h2>
  <p>If you write capabilities yourself in the repo (without the skill), that still expects Rust: implement the logic, attach the contract metadata, and compile to <code>wasm32-wasip1</code>. The expedition planning example is a good reference for that path.</p>

  <h2>What you need in each role</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plain-English author:</strong> Claude (or another client that can load the skill), a description of the business rule, and willingness to review the PR the skill opens. No Rust required of you.</li>
    <li><strong>Capability consumer:</strong> npm embedder for JS/TS, or <code>traverse-cli</code> via subprocess for Python. No Rust, no WASM tooling in your app code.</li>
    <li><strong>Manual capability author:</strong> Rust toolchain (rustup), the <code>wasm32-wasip1</code> target, and familiarity with writing Rust functions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>See the <a href="/faq.html">FAQ</a>, the <a href="/questions/how-do-i-install-traverse.html">installation guide</a>, the <a href="/questions/how-do-i-author-a-capability-in-plain-english.html">plain-English authoring</a> page, or the <a href="/contribute.html">contribute</a> page for where to start.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
